-- title:
--   Preprocessing inherits the feasibility contract’s resource bounds
-- statement:
--   Write $\operatorname{oc}(P)$ for the sum of the two original-oracle charge counters of a computation $P$, and $\operatorname{work}(P)$ for its fair-bit and named nonoracle primitive charges. Fix a feasibility contract: an oracle-based Boolean solver $F$, natural constants $A,d_A,B,d_B$, correctness for full-ground equal-rank matroids represented by exact independence oracles, and uniform bounds $\operatorname{oc}(F)\le A(n+r+1)^{d_A}$ and $\operatorname{work}(F)\le B(n+r+1)^{d_B}$ for every input and every pair of Boolean oracles. Correctness means that $F$ answers true exactly when the represented matroids have a common basis. Let $n,r\in\mathbb N$ and let $o_1,o_2$ be arbitrary Boolean functions on subset queries of $E_n=\{0,\ldots,n-1\}$. The preprocessing routine $P$ returns $1$ immediately for $n=0$ and otherwise invokes $F$, returning no preliminary answer on true and $0$ on false. It satisfies $$\operatorname{oc}(P)\le A(n+r+1)^{d_A},\qquad\operatorname{work}(P)\le B(n+r+1)^{d_B}+1.$$
-- source:
--   https://github.com/meelgroup/counting-matroid/blob/dd16a4628fdb095c58fe0f3dfd8e353c6e97443c/CountingMatroid/Analysis/ResourceBound.lean#L113-L134

